WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.963
Square-root decomposition splits an array of size N into blocks of size roughly square root of N.

00:00:06.963 --> 00:00:14.859
Range queries inspect at most N over B full blocks in O(1) each and at most 2B boundary elements individually.

00:00:14.859 --> 00:00:21.320
Choosing B near square root of N balances both terms at O(square root of N) per operation.

00:00:21.583 --> 00:00:31.198
When range queries are offline and have no updates, Mo's algorithm reorders them by left-endpoint block and then by right endpoint.

00:00:31.198 --> 00:00:41.326
Alternating the R sort direction between even and odd blocks prevents the right pointer from jumping back across the array between blocks.

00:00:41.326 --> 00:00:46.463
Both pointers move at most O((N + Q) square root of N) steps in total.

00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:56.204
Move the current range boundaries one step at a time, always expanding L and R outward before shrinking them inward so the interval never inverts.

00:00:56.204 --> 00:01:03.974
In Python, inline the add and remove updates using flat integer lists rather than dictionaries or helper function calls.

00:01:03.974 --> 00:01:08.830
Constant-time transitions are essential because they run millions of times.

00:01:09.083 --> 00:01:16.846
On graphs or frequency tables, classify items with count above square root of M as heavy and the rest as light.

00:01:16.846 --> 00:01:25.168
Light items can be iterated directly on update, while there are at most square root of M heavy items to check on query.

00:01:25.168 --> 00:01:29.574
Heavy-light thresholding bounds worst-case star graphs cleanly.
